# Repo-scoped allowlist. Every term here is a NAMED PATTERN from a citable
# primary source, not AI slop vocabulary. Blocking them would make the
# corresponding family uncitable. Each carries its source.
#
# discovery         Richardson, Microservices Patterns (Manning 2018).
#                   "Service Discovery", "Client-side Discovery",
#                   "Server-side Discovery". microservices.io/patterns/
# context engineering
#                   Anthropic engineering, "Effective context engineering for
#                   AI agents". A named practice in family 17.
discovery
context engineering
# context window. A real technical term in family 17 (lost-in-the-middle,
#   long context versus RAG) and in this repo's own operating notes.
context window
# decode / decoded. Python's bytes.decode() and analogous encode/decode
# terminology are literal stdlib method names in the gateway-offloading
# code examples (compression, TLS byte streams), not AI-slop prose.
decode
decoded
# end-to-end. The named technical term for the terminate-and-re-encrypt TLS
# variant, used verbatim by Microsoft Learn's own Azure Application Gateway
# documentation ("end to end TLS"). Not startup-jargon slop, a cited network
# security term central to the gateway-offloading pattern.
end-to-end
# cadence. The named term for release/deploy frequency in Newman's
# Building Microservices and the DORA deploy-frequency metric, central to
# the self-contained-service entry's deploy-independence discussion.
cadence
# freedom. Newman's own chapter language for per-service technology choice,
# a cited engineering trade-off, not startup-jargon slop.
freedom
# just. Plain English adverb of degree/limitation used repeatedly in
# ordinary technical prose (just moves the coupling problem up a layer),
# not the hedge-word this list otherwise targets.
just
# meaningful. Plain English adjective describing a UI seam or a workflow a
# user experiences, not AI-slop importance-inflation.
meaningful
# structurally. Plain adverb distinguishing a structural (enforced by the
# pattern itself) constraint from a process convention.
structurally
# typically. Plain hedge adverb stating a common-but-not-universal fact,
# needed for honest, non-absolute claims per the judgement-vs-sourced-claim
# contract in ENTRY-TEMPLATE.md.
typically
